ver the more tactile familiar  textures of models and makeup every star  wars opening scene works best as a  preview of the style and thematic  concerns that the rest of the film will  follow and phantom menace for better or  worse is exactly that  the last of the original trilogy  premiered in 1983 and return of the jedi  is a film whose structure bears more  than a little resemblance to the first  star wars film as in the original film  jedi spends much of its first act on the  desert planet tatooine and climaxes with  the rebels victory over the empire's  newly constructed death star one gets  the sense that lucas wanted a do-over on  certain parts of a new hope as if he  wanted to take advantage of the  technological and budgetary leaps made  since 1977 he effectively redoes the mos  eisley cantina scene with a far more  complex menagerie of aliens in jabba the  hunt's palace and he stages a bigger  busier death star battle than was  possible the first time around jedi's  opening scene establishes a new under  construction death star it also  establishes that darth vader is back in  a role as a subordinate as he was with  grandma turken and a new hope as he  arrives on the death star vader reveals  to the commander that the emperor will  be coming to oversee the completion of  the base it's an ominous scene that sets  up the climactic stakes of the last film  in the original trilogy that is correct  commander star wars was risking serious  audience fatigue when it released solo a  star wars story less than six months  after the last jedi not only that but  the production went through a  high-profile shake-up when original  directors phil lord and chris miller  were fired and replaced by filmmaker ron  howard resulting in well-publicized  reshoots if that weren't enough the film  was attempting to recast harrison ford's  han solo one of the most iconic pairings  of actor and character in film history  the resulting film is torn between  delivering a fun space adventure and  hitting every recognizable part of han  solo's backstory it bounces from  important moments like meeting his  wookie co-pilot chewbacca and winning  the millennium falcon from frenemy lando  calrissian to trivial beats like how he  got his blaster in the opening scene  howard directs the speeder chase through  the broken down shipyards of jung han's  home planet the scene is set to a  rousing and romantic new theme from  composer john williams but it becomes  bogged down by references to thermal  detonators the gold dice that will one  day hang in the falcon's cockpit and the  laughably unnecessary moment in which  han's clearly symbolic last name is  bestowed upon him  attack of the clones opens with an  explosion that rocks queen turned  senator padme amidala's ship as it lands  on the republic capital planet coruscant  the identity of the bomber and their  reasons for wanting padme dead are  treated as an important mystery as a  result obi-wan kenobi plays detective  while his apprentice anakin skywalker  violates his jedi oath by falling in  love with padme while serving as her  bodyguard  intrigue is not a color that star wars  paints with very often and it's  particularly difficult with prequels  audiences know from the start that the  ultimate culprit is newly elected  supreme chancellor palpatine who will  use a phony war to consolidate power on  the way to becoming emperor and destroy  the jedi still attack of the clones  leans into a 1940s detective vibe even  down to a bizarre retro diner where  kenobi gains a vital clue but while it's  fun to imagine a full-on film noir star  wars the movie ultimately isn't  interested in sustaining that vibe an  attack of the clones soon returns to the  more is more aesthetic that marks the  prequel trilogy as a whole  the second star wars film produced by  disney 2016's bleak prequel rogue one a  star wars story was beset by reshoots  and behind the scenes shakeups just as  solo would be two years later but the  finished film hangs together remarkably  well as they start consideration of  wartime sacrifice that affords no  last-minute rescue for its doomed rebel  spies  a tone is set in the film's opening  scene a flashback in which the pastoral  life of young gin urso and her family is  shattered by the arrival of imperial  troops her father galen was an imperial  scientist whose moral objections to  working on the death star forced him  into hiding but now the empire  represented by the slimy orson krennic  has come calling once again  you're hard man to find galen  but farming  young jin flees to an underground bunker  as her mother lira is shot down by  stormtroopers and galen is taken away to  finish his work on the deadly space  station the scene effectively lays out  the narrative and emotional stakes of  the rest of the film though it's also a  prime example of the disney tendency to  have everything important to a character  happen all at once  after a prequel trilogy's worth of trade  federation separatists count dooku and  general grievous jj abrams 2015 sequel  the force awakens announces its back to  the basics intentions in the first line  of the title crawl  here the original trilogy's characters  have become legends and inspirations to  the series new characters  when ace x-wing pilot poe dameron refers  to leia by her newly established rank of  general an old rebel ally smiles warmly  and replies for the general  to me she's royalty the opening scene  concerns the battle for a star map that  will reveal luke skywalker's whereabouts  and it structurally serves to introduce  most of the sequel trilogy's new  characters there's poe a hot shot pilot  whose cavalier personality gets him into  a fair bit of trouble we then meet dark  lord kylo ren who attacks lor san  tekka's desert village in search of the  star map next there's the conflicted  stormtrooper finn whose first taste of  combat leaves him traumatized and  looking for a way out then there's bb-8  a next-generation r2d2 with more  charisma and personality than many of  the prequel trilogy's human performers  only rey is missing from the sequence  just like luke in a new hope she shows  up after the intense opening action  sequence  after two films of political  conspiracies 2005's revenge of the sith  finally lands viewers in the middle of  an actual star war as obi-wan kenobi and  anakin skywalker raced to rescue  chancellor palpatine from the clutches  of sith lord count dooku and the  four-armed jedi killing droid general  grievous little do they know palpatine  is in fact dooku's evil master and that  the kidnapping was staged as part of his  years-long plan to rule the galaxy star  wars rarely takes full advantage of the  three-dimensionality of outer space  which makes it a pleasant surprise when  the camera pans down from obi-wan and  anakin's solitary interceptors to settle  on the gigantic battle raging just below  them hayden christensen still struggles  to infuse anakin with much charm or  passion but overall the film is  invigorated by being the final entry in  the prequel trilogy and last lucas can  show all the things audiences have been  waiting for from the fall of the  republic to the rise of darth vader  star wars has had its share of humor  often powered by the charisma of its  actors but it rarely tries to tell jokes  and among the types of jokes that a star  wars film would try to tell a prank  phone call would certainly be one of the  more unexpected but that's exactly what  happens at the start of ryan johnson's  the last jedi in the sequence poe damer  installs for time while his rocket  boosted x-wing powers up he hails the  first order commander general hux and  then pretends not to hear him as hux  blusters about in confusion one of his  captains has to explain the joke i  believe he's tooling with you sir but  johnson has more on his mind than making  star wars intentionally funny again as  seen in the rest of the opening scene 